TASK Deprecated {}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 2, version 2.0.0*

### `Generate`

**Grammar:** `generation ::= "GENERATE" "(" gen_attr_list? ")" "->" IDENT ":" gen_type
gen_attr_list ::= gen_attr ("," gen_attr)*
gen_attr ::= "quantity" "=" UNSIGNED_INT | IDENT "=" value
UNSIGNED_INT ::= "0" | [1-9][0-9]*
gen_type ::= "NUMBER" | "STRING" | "BOOL" | "LIST" "[" gen_type "]"
-- A quantity attribute, if present, must use the quantity production above; quantity=NUMBER through the generic attribute production is a static error.`

**Semantics:** GENERATE is a pure, non-effectful construct that invokes a generative model to produce a value without external side effects. It shares Action's canonical attribute vocabulary and duplicate-attribute prohibition. target is always the requested output artifact, never content; content is the source instruction or text payload; audience is the intended reader; recipient is forbidden. Its optional tone and register_note follow Action's shared tone normalization rules and are mutually exclusive.

A GENERATE result type must be a gen_type: NUMBER, STRING, BOOL, or a finite nesting of LIST around those types. REF[T] is prohibited at every nesting depth: GENERATE may not declare REF[T], LIST[REF[T]], or any type containing REF. This restriction follows the global REF rule: generated text or values cannot manufacture an opaque runtime identity. The generator must return a value exactly matching its declared gen_type, including homogeneous recursively valid list elements.

quantity is an exact output-size constraint, not a maximum, minimum, estimate, or preference. Its lexical form is UNSIGNED_INT only: it may have no sign, decimal point, exponent, whitespace, or leading zero except for the literal 0. quantity is legal only for results declared STRING or LIST[T]. For LIST[T], the result contains exactly quantity elements. For STRING, it contains exactly quantity words. A word is a maximal non-empty sequence of Unicode characters whose General_Category is a Letter category (L*) or Decimal_Number (Nd); every other Unicode character is a separator. Thus quantity=0 on STRING requires a string containing no such sequence, and quantity=0 on LIST[T] requires an empty list. The executor validates the declared type and quantity condition before completion; inability to produce a conforming result fails the step under the general external-failure rule.

GENERATE must always bind its result using -> IDENT : gen_type. Use GENERATE to produce an artifact for the agent environment; use CONVO and UTTER to record observed dialogue or dialogue history.

**Example:**
- NL: "Give the user an opinion about taxes in exactly 50 words."
- BrainCode: `ENTRYPOINT TaxesOpinion

TASK TaxesOpinion : STRING {
  GENERATE(target="response", audience="user", content="Respond to the user's view that taxes are bad.", quantity=50) -> response : STRING
  RETURN response
}`

*Introduced/last revised: sprint 7, version 6.0.0*


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | CODE | PROPOSITION | ENTITY | ROLE | VALUE | LIST "[" type "]" | REF "[" type "]"
value ::= literal | entity_symbol | role_symbol | value_symbol | HANDLE | list_lit | proposition | code | diff
literal ::= NUMBER_LITERAL | TRUE | FALSE | DATE_LITERAL | TIME_LITERAL | URL_LITERAL | PATH_LITERAL | NAME_LITERAL
list_lit ::= "[" value ("," value)* "]"
diff ::= HANDLE "-" NUMBER_LITERAL
proposition ::= intent_symbol "(" proposition_arg_list? ")"
proposition_arg_list ::= proposition_arg ("," proposition_arg)*
proposition_arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value
code ::= code_node
ontology_symbol ::= operation_symbol | intent_symbol | entity_symbol | role_symbol | value_symbol | attribute_symbol
HANDLE ::= "$v" POSITIVE_INTEGER
POSITIVE_INTEGER ::= [1-9][0-9]*
NUMBER_LITERAL ::= "-"? ("0" | [1-9][0-9]*) ( "." [0-9]+ )?
DATE_LITERAL ::= "@d" YYYY "-" MM "-" DD
YYYY ::= [0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]
MM ::= "0"[1-9] | "1"[0-2]
DD ::= "0"[1-9] | [1-2][0-9] | "3"[0-1]
TIME_LITERAL ::= "@t" HH ":" MM ":" SS "Z"
HH ::= [0-1][0-9] | "2"[0-3]
SS ::= [0-5][0-9]
URL_LITERAL ::= "@u" URL_BODY
URL_BODY ::= [a-zA-Z0-9\-._~:/?#\[\]@!$&'()*+,;=%]+
PATH_LITERAL ::= "@p" PATH_BODY
PATH_BODY ::= ("/"? [a-zA-Z0-9_\-.]+)+
NAME_LITERAL ::= "@n" NAME_BODY
NAME_BODY ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_\-]*
WHITESPACE ::= [ \t\n\r]+
COMMENT ::= "--" [^\n]* "\n"
-- Tokens are separated by WHITESPACE or delimiters ( ) { } [ ] , = : - ->. Ontology categories are namespace-disjoint: op_, int_, ent_, role_, val_, attr_.` — BrainCode has no STRING type and no quoted-text syntax. Every ontology symbol has one category-defined static type. DATE_LITERAL must be a valid proleptic-Gregorian date. TIME_LITERAL is UTC. URL_LITERAL is an RFC-3986 absolute URI. PATH_LITERAL is a normalized path. NAME_LITERAL is a single atomic proper-name token. HANDLE allocation is mandatory and canonical: traverse the finalized document preorder, assigning $v1 to the ENTRYPOINT target, then each declaration name, parameter, result binding, turn name, loop binding, and LET binding at its first introduction in source order. A later reference must use its allocated handle. The ENTRYPOINT target handle must identically match the declared task or conversation handle.
- [add vocabulary] structural-symbol (closed): Structural symbols are the complete finite set of BrainCode keywords, operators, delimiters, type names, and literal prefixes. Members: ENTRYPOINT, TASK, ANCHOR, ACTION, UTTER, GENERATE, CONVO, TURN, SPEAKER, USER, AGENT, REPLY_TO, REVISES, IF, THEN, ELSE, FOR, EACH, IN, ANY, ALL, SATISFIES, LET, RETURN, DECREASES, AND, OR, NOT, TRUE, FALSE, NUMBER, BOOL, DATE, TIME, URL, PATH, NAME, CODE, PROPOSITION, ENTITY, ROLE, VALUE, LIST, REF, == != < <= > >=, -> = : -, ( ) { } [ ] ,, $v @d @t @u @p @n, code_call code_assign code_assert_equal code_identifier code_string code_number code_type
- [add vocabulary] literal-form (open): Literal forms define the few open-ended atomic values that may appear directly in source. Members: NUMBER_LITERAL, DATE_LITERAL, TIME_LITERAL, URL_LITERAL, PATH_LITERAL, NAME_LITERAL, HANDLE; membership rule: A literal is a member only when it matches exactly one lexical production in Types & Lexicon.
- [add vocabulary] code-form (closed): Code-form represents source code and exact program outputs as typed symbolic trees. Members: code_call, code_assign, code_assert_equal, code_identifier, code_string, code_number, code_type; membership rule: code_node ::= code_call(attr_callee=CODE, attr_args=LIST[CODE]) | code_assign(attr_target=CODE, attr_value=CODE) | code_assert_equal(attr_expected=CODE, attr_actual=CODE) | code_identifier(attr_name=NAME_LITERAL) | code_string(attr_text=NAME_LITERAL) | code_number(attr_value=NUMBER_LITERAL) | code_type(attr_type=ENTITY). No hexadecimal encoding is permitted. Code nodes strictly use namespace-valid attribute symbols.
- [add vocabulary] entity-symbol (open): Entity symbols name objects, places, systems, documents, and domain concepts. Members: ent_pillow, ent_sofa, ent_armchair, ent_mug, ent_sink, ent_coffee_maker, ent_tv_show, ent_netflix, ent_dog_collar, ent_apple, ent_apple_slice, ent_microwave, ent_table, ent_corner_table, ent_logistic_regression, ent_foreign_key, ent_django, ent_netherlands, ent_supreme_court_justice_appointment, ent_aotearoa, ent_politics, ent_dam_construction, ent_seasonal_precipitation_pattern, ent_land_use_cover, ent_socio_economic, ent_eastern_sudan, ent_the_brothers_karamazov, ent_the_holy_blood_and_the_holy_grail, ent_gloopy, ent_glitter, ent_scientist, ent_gloopiter, ent_hamoudi_educational_youtube_channel, ent_preschool_children, ent_japan, ent_sgi, ent_eastern_cape_south_africa, ent_safari, ent_united_kingdom, ent_mortgage_eligibility, ent_paris, ent_lyon, ent_berlin, ent_italy; membership rule: An entity symbol has exact form ent_[a-z0-9_]+ and is valid only when the complete glossary contains a corresponding entry.
- [add vocabulary] role-symbol (open): Role symbols name recipients, participants, and relational positions. Members: role_manager, role_user, role_agent, role_president, role_speaker; membership rule: A role symbol has exact form role_[a-z0-9_]+.
- [add vocabulary] value-symbol (open): Value symbols are controlled values used by attributes. Members: val_comedy, val_audience_score_desc, val_usd, val_rating_desc, val_unheated, val_makemigrations_crash, val_place_recommendations, val_great, val_year, val_distraction_from_pain, val_current, val_semi_arid, val_introduction, val_conspiracy_like, val_interpretive_ideas, val_amazed_at_speech, val_story, val_speaking_engagement_contract, val_revised_legal_contract, val_growth, val_posting_schedule, val_word, val_personal_text, val_day, val_safari_plan, val_short, val_stable_employment, val_certification_examples, val_polite; membership rule: A value symbol has exact form val_[a-z0-9_]+. It defines permissible values for attributes like tone, unit, ordering, currency, state, goal, and format.
- [add vocabulary] attribute-symbol (open): Attribute symbols label the named parts of an operation or proposition. Members: attr_target, attr_source, attr_quantity, attr_destination, attr_recipient, attr_deadline, attr_path, attr_change, attr_platform, attr_genre, attr_ordering, attr_max_price, attr_currency, attr_state, attr_subject, attr_reproduction, attr_expected, attr_actual, attr_framework, attr_failure, attr_quality, attr_duration, attr_unit, attr_purpose, attr_appointer, attr_location, attr_topic, attr_year, attr_requirement, attr_effects, attr_climate, attr_work_a, attr_work_b, attr_theme, attr_characters, attr_event, attr_participants, attr_reaction, attr_setting, attr_document_type, attr_binding, attr_author_role, attr_audience, attr_goal, attr_actor, attr_decision, attr_length, attr_activity, attr_context, attr_tone, attr_content, attr_callee, attr_args, attr_value, attr_name, attr_type, attr_specification, attr_date, attr_text; membership rule: An attribute symbol has exact form attr_[a-z0-9_]+ and defines the admissible value types for the attribute.
- [add vocabulary] operation-symbol (open): Operation symbols name external operations and define their complete schemas and canonical decomposition rules. Members: op_find, op_place, op_send_email, op_check_replied, op_modify_code, op_rinse, op_slice, op_heat, op_extract, op_sort, op_search_travel; membership rule: A member has exact form op_[a-z0-9_]+. Defines operation schemas and selection rules to resolve ambiguities (e.g., sort-plus-extract vs ordered find).
- [add vocabulary] intent-symbol (open): Intent symbols name structured communicative, generative, and informational meanings. Members: int_request_extension, int_follow_up, int_greet, int_bug_report, int_fix_mixed_case_app_reference, int_recommend_places, int_report_pain, int_request_joke, int_question_fairness, int_request_current_events, int_challenge_information_currency, int_introduce_topic, int_compare_themes, int_generate_story, int_first_meeting, int_revise_document, int_add_signature_line, int_plan_posting_schedule, int_explain_personal_decision, int_travel, int_learn_about, int_plan_trip, int_recommend_certifications; membership rule: A member has exact form int_[a-z0-9_]+. Defines intent schemas and selection rules (e.g., TASK/GENERATE vs live find vs CONVO).
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= ACTION operation_symbol "(" action_attr_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?
action_attr_list ::= action_attr ("," action_attr)*
action_attr ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — ACTION is the sole external-state construct. Its operation must be a glossary-defined operation symbol with a complete ordered schema. Required, optional, duplicate, out-of-order, extra, and type-incompatible attributes are static errors. No default operation signature exists. Result handles use the global allocation rule.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol "(" utterance_attr_list? ")" ("->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION)?
utterance_attr_list ::= utterance_attr ("," utterance_attr)*
utterance_attr ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — UTTER records a non-effectful speech act and is legal only in a CONVO TURN body. Its intent symbol supplies the complete ordered schema. No content string, quoted phrase, register note, or unrestricted identifier is legal. An optional result binds the complete PROPOSITION using the canonical next handle.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE "(" generation_attr_list ")" "->" HANDLE ":" gen_type
generation_attr_list ::= generation_attr ("," generation_attr)*
generation_attr ::= attribute_symbol "=" value
gen_type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | CODE | PROPOSITION | LIST "[" gen_type "]"` — GENERATE is pure symbolic derivation. It requires a glossary-defined attr_target and attr_specification, where specification is PROPOSITION or CODE according to its generation schema. It cannot create REF values, quoted prompts, text fragments, or free-form output strings. Its use is selected only when the source asks for a derived artifact rather than a live external operation or an observed conversation turn.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*
task ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (":" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? "{" step+ return_stmt? "}"
param_list ::= "(" param ("," param)* ")"
param ::= HANDLE ":" type
return_stmt ::= RETURN value
step ::= action | utterance | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation
task_call ::= HANDLE arg_list? ("->" HANDLE ":" type)?
arg_list ::= "(" arg ("," arg)* ")"
arg ::= attribute_symbol "=" value` — A document has one ENTRYPOINT naming a declared Task or Conversation by its canonical HANDLE. The target handle and the declared handle must match identically. TASK ANCHOR is optional only when the source request contains no relative temporal expression. When a source contains relative time, its enclosing Task must carry exactly one ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL. `step` includes UTTER so Turn bodies are grammatically valid; however, UTTER is statically illegal in a TASK body and legal only in a CONVO TURN body. All declarations, parameters, results, arguments, and calls use canonical handles or schema-authorized symbols.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET HANDLE ":" type "=" value
-- Additional bind sites: action_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; task_call_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" type ; utterance_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" PROPOSITION ; generation_result ::= "->" HANDLE ":" gen_type` — Bind gives a typed value an opaque local HANDLE. A HANDLE is not an entity name, noun, English descriptor, or ontology symbol; it is a source-local address whose spelling is generated by literal-form. Handles may be introduced only at Bind sites, task and turn declarations, parameters, and loop bind sites. Shadowing, use before binding, and incompatible types remain static errors. REF values can be copied but never manufactured from a literal or ontology symbol.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE "{" turn+ "}"
turn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER "=" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? "{" step+ "}"
speaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT` — Conversation is an ordered symbolic dialogue. TURN bodies use Task's shared step production, including UTTER, but the placement rule permits UTTER only here. TURN handles follow global preorder allocation. REPLY_TO and REVISES must name earlier turn handles in the same conversation. Revision remains whole-body replacement along a single tip-only chain. Every utterance uses an intent schema and contains no prose payload.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow_if ::= IF check THEN "{" step+ "}" (ELSE "{" step+ "}")?` — Conditional branching retains its existing evaluation, scope, and ordering rules. Every value used by its Check must be a glossary-valid value under Types & Lexicon; in particular, no check may compare or test a quoted string or undefined identifier. Deferred external checks must use a TIME_LITERAL, DATE_LITERAL, or glossary-defined temporal value accepted by their operation signature.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable "{" step+ "}"
iterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit
quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check` — FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain their existing finite-list, sequential evaluation, scope, and no-shadowing behavior. Loop and quantifier variables are HANDLEs, so they are opaque formal bindings rather than English-derived names. List elements must be glossary-valid values of one homogeneous static type.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction
disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*
conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*
negation ::= NOT negation | primary
primary ::= "(" check ")" | quantifier | comparison
comparison ::= value (comp_op value)?
comp_op ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="` — Check remains a pure typed boolean expression with the established precedence, short-circuiting, truthiness, and comparison rules. Its operands are now restricted to Types & Lexicon values: atomic literals, glossary symbols, handles, lists, and propositions. PROPOSITION supports only equality and inequality, comparing the intent symbol and canonically ordered attribute sequence recursively. No free string, quoted phrase, or undeclared identifier can be an operand.

Task: "Please provide a recipe for high protein flatbread"

Return JSON: {"braincode": "..."}

--- RESPONSE ---
{"braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\n\nTASK $v1 : PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(attr_target=ent_recipe, attr_specification=int_request_recipe(attr_subject=ent_flatbread, attr_requirement=val_high_protein)) -> $v2 : PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}"}

