yms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_args", "gloss": "Args. Type: LIST[CODE].", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_value", "gloss": "Value. Type: CODE | NUMBER.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_name", "gloss": "Name. Type: NAME.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_type", "gloss": "Type. Type: ENTITY.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_specification", "gloss": "Specification. Type: PROPOSITION.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_date", "gloss": "Date. Type: DATE.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "attr_text", "gloss": "Text content. Type: NAME.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "An attribute symbol has exact form attr_[a-z0-9_]+ and defines the admissible value types for the attribute.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Request an extension for two days.", "braincode": "int_request_extension(attr_duration=2, attr_unit=val_day)"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Explicitly lists every attribute symbol used in schemas along with its allowed value types.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "operation-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Operation symbols name external operations and define their complete schemas and canonical decomposition rules.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "op_find", "gloss": "Finds entities. Required ordered attributes: attr_target. Optional: attr_source, attr_platform, attr_genre, attr_max_price, attr_currency, attr_state, attr_ordering, attr_quantity. Returns LIST[REF[ENTITY]] or REF[ENTITY]. Canonical rule: Live search must use op_find. Filters, sorting, and extraction must be encoded as attributes on op_find (e.g. attr_ordering) rather than separate operations if the target platform supports them in a single query.", "nl_synonyms": ["find"]}, {"symbol": "op_place", "gloss": "Places entity. Required: attr_target, attr_destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["put"]}, {"symbol": "op_send_email", "gloss": "Sends email. Required: attr_recipient, attr_content.", "nl_synonyms": ["email"]}, {"symbol": "op_check_replied", "gloss": "Checks reply. Required: attr_target, attr_deadline.", "nl_synonyms": ["check reply"]}, {"symbol": "op_modify_code", "gloss": "Modifies code. Required: attr_path, attr_change.", "nl_synonyms": ["fix"]}, {"symbol": "op_rinse", "gloss": "Rinses entity. Required: attr_target, attr_destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["rinse"]}, {"symbol": "op_slice", "gloss": "Slices entity. Required: attr_target. Returns LIST[REF[ENTITY]]. Canonical rule: Slicing must use op_slice, not op_find.", "nl_synonyms": ["slice"]}, {"symbol": "op_heat", "gloss": "Heats entity. Required: attr_target, attr_destination.", "nl_synonyms": ["microwave"]}, {"symbol": "op_extract", "gloss": "Extracts from list. Required: attr_target, attr_quantity.", "nl_synonyms": ["extract"]}, {"symbol": "op_sort", "gloss": "Sorts list. Required: attr_target, attr_ordering.", "nl_synonyms": ["sort"]}, {"symbol": "op_search_travel", "gloss": "Searches travel. Required: attr_destination. Optional: attr_date.", "nl_synonyms": ["search flights"]}], "membership_rule": "A member has exact form op_[a-z0-9_]+. Defines operation schemas and selection rules to resolve ambiguities (e.g., sort-plus-extract vs ordered find).", "worked_example": {"nl": "Find the highest rated dog collar under 10 dollar.", "braincode": "ACTION op_find(attr_target=ent_dog_collar, attr_max_price=10, attr_currency=val_usd, attr_ordering=val_rating_desc, attr_quantity=1) -> $v2:REF[ENTITY]"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Defines operations and explicit canonical selection rules for finding vs sorting/extracting.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "add", "kind": "vocabulary", "construct_name": "intent-symbol", "glossary_gloss": "Intent symbols name structured communicative, generative, and informational meanings.", "closed": false, "members": [{"symbol": "int_request_extension", "gloss": "Requests extension.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_follow_up", "gloss": "Follows up.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_greet", "gloss": "Greets.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_bug_report", "gloss": "Bug report. Attributes: attr_subject, attr_failure, attr_context, attr_reproduction, attr_expected, attr_actual.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_fix_mixed_case_app_reference", "gloss": "Fix mixed case app reference.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_recommend_places", "gloss": "Recommend places.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_report_pain", "gloss": "Report pain.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_request_joke", "gloss": "Request joke.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_question_fairness", "gloss": "Question fairness.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_request_current_events", "gloss": "Request current events.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_challenge_information_currency", "gloss": "Challenge information currency.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_introduce_topic", "gloss": "Introduce topic.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_compare_themes", "gloss": "Compare themes.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_generate_story", "gloss": "Generate story.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_first_meeting", "gloss": "First meeting.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_revise_document", "gloss": "Revise document. Canonical rule: Revision of a generated document uses GENERATE with int_revise_document, not CONVO/REVISES, unless observing a human correction of a dialogue turn.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_add_signature_line", "gloss": "Add signature line.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_plan_posting_schedule", "gloss": "Plan posting schedule.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_explain_personal_decision", "gloss": "Explain personal decision.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_travel", "gloss": "Travel.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_learn_about", "gloss": "Learn about.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_plan_trip", "gloss": "Plan trip.", "nl_synonyms": []}, {"symbol": "int_recommend_certifications", "gloss": "Recommend certifications.", "nl_synonyms": []}], "membership_rule": "A member has exact form int_[a-z0-9_]+. Defines intent schemas and selection rules (e.g., TASK/GENERATE vs live find vs CONVO).", "worked_example": {"nl": "Add a binding signature line.", "braincode": "GENERATE(attr_target=val_revised_legal_contract, attr_specification=int_revise_document(attr_document_type=val_speaking_engagement_contract, attr_change=int_add_signature_line(attr_binding=TRUE), attr_author_role=role_speaker)) -> $v2:PROPOSITION"}, "change_type": "MINOR", "rationale": "Provides the required intent schemas and selection rules for generation vs external actions.", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action ::= ACTION operation_symbol \"(\" action_attr_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?\naction_attr_list ::= action_attr (\",\" action_attr)*\naction_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value", "semantics": "ACTION is the sole external-state construct. Its operation must be a glossary-defined operation symbol with a complete ordered schema. Required, optional, duplicate, out-of-order, extra, and type-incompatible attributes are static errors. No default operation signature exists. Result handles use the global allocation rule.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Fix the file using a structured code change.", "braincode": "ACTION op_modify_code(attr_path=@psklearn/linear_model/logistic.py, attr_change=int_bug_report(attr_subject=ent_logistic_regression, attr_expected=code_call(attr_callee=code_identifier(attr_name=@nLogisticRegression), attr_args=[code_assign(attr_target=code_identifier(attr_name=@nmulti_class), attr_value=code_identifier(attr_name=@nmultinomial))])))"}, "glossary_gloss": "Action performs one glossary-defined external operation using a fixed symbolic schema. It cannot accept free text or unknown attributes.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Action must accept structured code evidence where required while retaining fully defined operation and argument vocabularies.", "cites_source": "Why Agentic Systems Need Ontologies", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Utterance", "grammar": "utterance ::= UTTER intent_symbol \"(\" utterance_attr_list? \")\" (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION)?\nutterance_attr_list ::= utterance_attr (\",\" utterance_attr)*\nutterance_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value", "semantics": "UTTER records a non-effectful speech act and is legal only in a CONVO TURN body. Its intent symbol supplies the complete ordered schema. No content string, quoted phrase, register note, or unrestricted identifier is legal. An optional result binds the complete PROPOSITION using the canonical next handle.", "worked_example": {"nl": "User says hello.", "braincode": "CONVO $v1 { TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_greet() } }"}, "glossary_gloss": "Utterance records one symbolic thing said in a conversation. Its meaning comes from an intent and its defined attributes, not from embedded prose.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Revalidates UTTER against the corrected Task and Conversation grammar and preserves N1/N4 compliance.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Generate", "grammar": "generation ::= GENERATE \"(\" generation_attr_list \")\" \"->\" HANDLE \":\" gen_type\ngeneration_attr_list ::= generation_attr (\",\" generation_attr)*\ngeneration_attr ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value\ngen_type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | DATE | TIME | URL | PATH | NAME | CODE | PROPOSITION | LIST \"[\" gen_type \"]\"", "semantics": "GENERATE is pure symbolic derivation. It requires a glossary-defined attr_target and attr_specification, where specification is PROPOSITION or CODE according to its generation schema. It cannot create REF values, quoted prompts, text fragments, or free-form output strings. Its use is selected only when the source asks for a derived artifact rather than a live external operation or an observed conversation turn.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Generate a symbolic trip plan for Italy.", "braincode": "GENERATE(attr_target=val_safari_plan, attr_specification=int_plan_trip(attr_destination=ent_italy)) -> $v2:PROPOSITION"}, "glossary_gloss": "Generate creates a typed symbolic artifact from a typed symbolic specification. It never accepts an English prompt inside BrainCode.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Aligns generation with the canonical construct-selection rule and permits structured code artifacts without prose fallback.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "document ::= ENTRYPOINT HANDLE (task | conversation)*\ntask ::= TASK HANDLE param_list? (ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL)? (\":\" type)? (DECREASES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ return_stmt? \"}\"\nparam_list ::= \"(\" param (\",\" param)* \")\"\nparam ::= HANDLE \":\" type\nreturn_stmt ::= RETURN value\nstep ::= action | utterance | flow_if | flow_for | bind | task_call | generation\ntask_call ::= HANDLE arg_list? (\"->\" HANDLE \":\" type)?\narg_list ::= \"(\" arg (\",\" arg)* \")\"\narg ::= attribute_symbol \"=\" value", "semantics": "A document has one ENTRYPOINT naming a declared Task or Conversation by its canonical HANDLE. The target handle and the declared handle must match identically. TASK ANCHOR is optional only when the source request contains no relative temporal expression. When a source contains relative time, its enclosing Task must carry exactly one ANCHOR DATE_LITERAL. `step` includes UTTER so Turn bodies are grammatically valid; however, UTTER is statically illegal in a TASK body and legal only in a CONVO TURN body. All declarations, parameters, results, arguments, and calls use canonical handles or schema-authorized symbols.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If the manager has not replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 ANCHOR @d2026-09-30 {\n  ACTION op_check_replied(attr_target=role_manager, attr_deadline=@d2026-10-02) -> $v2:BOOL\n  IF NOT $v2 THEN { ACTION op_send_email(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_content=int_follow_up(attr_tone=val_polite)) }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Task is an ordered executable procedure with canonical opaque handles. Its optional date anchor makes relative-date translation reproducible and auditable.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Restores valid Turn use of UTTER and replaces lost temporal auditability with a typed, closed translation anchor. Fixes handle matching.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Bind", "grammar": "bind ::= LET HANDLE \":\" type \"=\" value\n-- Additional bind sites: action_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" type ; task_call_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" type ; utterance_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" PROPOSITION ; generation_result ::= \"->\" HANDLE \":\" gen_type", "semantics": "Bind gives a typed value an opaque local HANDLE. A HANDLE is not an entity name, noun, English descriptor, or ontology symbol; it is a source-local address whose spelling is generated by literal-form. Handles may be introduced only at Bind sites, task and turn declarations, parameters, and loop bind sites. Shadowing, use before binding, and incompatible types remain static errors. REF values can be copied but never manufactured from a literal or ontology symbol.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Remember the symbolic request for later use.", "braincode": "LET $v2 : PROPOSITION = int_request_extension(attr_duration=2, attr_unit=val_day)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Bind stores a typed symbolic value under an opaque local handle. The handle is only a formal reference and does not hide descriptive meaning in an English variable name.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The prior naming convention required English-derived identifiers such as mug and therefore made identifier semantics dependent on natural language. Opaque handles separate local reference from ontology meaning.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Conversation", "grammar": "conversation ::= CONVO HANDLE \"{\" turn+ \"}\"\nturn ::= TURN HANDLE SPEAKER \"=\" speaker_symbol (REPLY_TO HANDLE)? (REVISES HANDLE)? \"{\" step+ \"}\"\nspeaker_symbol ::= USER | AGENT", "semantics": "Conversation is an ordered symbolic dialogue. TURN bodies use Task's shared step production, including UTTER, but the placement rule permits UTTER only here. TURN handles follow global preorder allocation. REPLY_TO and REVISES must name earlier turn handles in the same conversation. Revision remains whole-body replacement along a single tip-only chain. Every utterance uses an intent schema and contains no prose payload.", "worked_example": {"nl": "The user asks for two days, then corrects the request to three days.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nCONVO $v1 {\n  TURN $v2 SPEAKER=USER { UTTER int_request_extension(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_duration=2, attr_unit=val_day) }\n  TURN $v3 SPEAKER=USER REVISES $v2 { UTTER int_request_extension(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_duration=3, attr_unit=val_day) }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Conversation records ordered symbolic turns, replies, and corrections. What each participant says is represented by an intent schema rather than quoted dialogue.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "The revised shared step grammar makes Conversation examples valid while retaining the required placement restriction on UTTER.", "cites_source": "Automatic Textbook Formalization", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Flow-If", "grammar": "flow_if ::= IF check THEN \"{\" step+ \"}\" (ELSE \"{\" step+ \"}\")?", "semantics": "Conditional branching retains its existing evaluation, scope, and ordering rules. Every value used by its Check must be a glossary-valid value under Types & Lexicon; in particular, no check may compare or test a quoted string or undefined identifier. Deferred external checks must use a TIME_LITERAL, DATE_LITERAL, or glossary-defined temporal value accepted by their operation signature.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If the manager has not replied by 6 June 2025, send a polite follow-up.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION op_check_replied(attr_target=role_manager, attr_deadline=@d2025-06-06) -> $v2 : BOOL\n  IF NOT $v2 THEN {\n    ACTION op_send_email(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_content=int_follow_up(attr_tone=val_polite))\n  }\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Flow-If chooses a symbolic branch using a typed formal check. It cannot test hidden text or use an undefined value.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Aligns all checked values with the closed lexical system.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Iterate", "grammar": "flow_for ::= FOR EACH HANDLE IN iterable \"{\" step+ \"}\"\niterable ::= HANDLE | list_lit\nquantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) HANDLE IN iterable SATISFIES check", "semantics": "FOR EACH, ANY, and ALL retain their existing finite-list, sequential evaluation, scope, and no-shadowing behavior. Loop and quantifier variables are HANDLEs, so they are opaque formal bindings rather than English-derived names. List elements must be glossary-valid values of one homogeneous static type.", "worked_example": {"nl": "For each city in the itinerary, search travel options.", "braincode": "LET $v2 : LIST[PROPOSITION] = [int_travel(attr_destination=ent_paris), int_travel(attr_destination=ent_lyon)]\nFOR EACH $v3 IN $v2 {\n  ACTION op_search_travel(attr_destination=$v3)\n}"}, "glossary_gloss": "Iterate processes each member of a formal finite list using an opaque loop handle. It does not introduce unlisted descriptive words through variable names or string lists.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Handle binding and symbolic list values make iteration conform to the closed vocabulary requirement.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Check", "grammar": "check ::= disjunction\ndisjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*\nconjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*\nnegation ::= NOT negation | primary\nprimary ::= \"(\" check \")\" | quantifier | comparison\ncomparison ::= value (comp_op value)?\ncomp_op ::= \"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \"<=\" | \">\" | \">=\"", "semantics": "Check remains a pure typed boolean expression with the established precedence, short-circuiting, truthiness, and comparison rules. Its operands are now restricted to Types & Lexicon values: atomic literals, glossary symbols, handles, lists, and propositions. PROPOSITION supports only equality and inequality, comparing the intent symbol and canonically ordered attribute sequence recursively. No free string, quoted phrase, or undeclared identifier can be an operand.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Proceed only if the total exceeds 50 and membership is true.", "braincode": "LET $v2 : NUMBER = 65\nLET $v3 : BOOL = TRUE\n$v2 > 50 AND $v3 == TRUE"}, "glossary_gloss": "Check combines typed formal values into a true-or-false condition. Its operands must be glossary-defined symbols, exact literals, or previously bound handles.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Updating its semantic domain ensures a condition cannot conceal undefined lexical material.", "cites_source": "Formal language theory (general)", "addresses_note": "N1"}], "_provider_used": "gemini", "_model_used": "gemini-3.1-pro-preview"}
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Typed entities, operations, intents, code nodes, and atomic literals improve coverage for simple placement, follow-up, and trip-planning tasks. However, many sampled actionable cores still have no schema: cart addition, acquisition/pick-up, rich code syntax and regressions, recipes, medical explanations, CRUD design advice, meal-prep plans, and detailed generated writing. Under N4, these cannot fall back to prose, so most open requests and several closed tasks fail outright."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal preserves some formerly opaque details, including quantities, dates, roles, structured calls, and a simple conversational correction. But it loses or cannot encode substantive requirements in the SWE-bench reports, compatibility constraints, high-review filtering, response duration, tone/persona composition, comedy-script constraints, sermon structure, and code output notation. `VALUE` is too broad and attributes do not enumerate allowed value sets, so the claimed schemas do not preserve all distinctions deterministically."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "Opaque canonical handles are an improvement, but operation and intent schemas are mostly absent or incomplete. Independent translations already disagree on whether to include the stated two-pillow quantity, on when to add cart items, on whether bug reports are Tasks or Conversations, and on whether a recipe request is generation or dialogue. `op_find` has an unspecified REF-versus-LIST result selection, generic VALUE admits arbitrary category members, and no canonical attribute order is supplied for most intent schemas."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Namespace prefixes and typed literals make simple expressions easier to inspect than unrestricted English identifiers and strings. Conversely, the proposal makes validity depend on an asserted but unavailable 'complete glossary,' uses external RFC-3986 as part of URL validity, permits arbitrary comment text, and leaves many category membership rules as prefix tests rather than glossary lookup. A reader cannot recover the intended value set or operation/intent schema from the supplied glossary entries."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "For simple procedural requests such as locating pillows and placing them sequentially, the structure would help a weaker model execute the plan. For the majority of sampled tasks, the language either has no valid expression or reduces the request to a coarse intent, so it cannot reliably improve planning over the original request."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION op_find(attr_target=ent_pillow, attr_source=ent_sofa, attr_quantity=2) -> $v2:LIST[REF[ENTITY]]\n  FOR EACH $v3 IN $v2 {\n    ACTION op_place(attr_target=$v3, attr_destination=ent_armchair)\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The entity, quantity, find, place, and sequential iteration vocabulary helps. `op_find` still does not specify why this result must be LIST rather than REF, so the expression is not canonically determined."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 ANCHOR @d2026-09-30 {\n  ACTION op_check_replied(attr_target=role_manager, attr_deadline=@d2026-10-02) -> $v2:BOOL\n  IF NOT $v2 THEN {\n    ACTION op_send_email(attr_recipient=role_manager, attr_content=int_follow_up(attr_tone=val_polite))\n  }\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The new symbolic content and anchor avoid prose. This assumes Friday relative to the supplied current date; the global translator anchor policy needs a normative source for the translation date."}, {"item_id": "e344b89e-767a-4618-ba82-4b81cdcba280", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor at any price to the shopping cart.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No `op_add_to_cart` exists. `attr_requirement` only accepts VALUE, so it cannot express compatibility with the selected motherboard reference; no compatibility relation schema exists."}, {"item_id": "f118238f-ef8f-4b63-9159-a81e981ef46e", "source": "Mind2Web", "nl": "Add decorative LED Candles to the cart.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Even assuming `ent_decorative_led_candles` belongs to the complete entity glossary, no cart-add operation or cart destination relation is defined."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_214148_552057#1", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "get a cup ready for coffee", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The trajectory requires retrieving a cup from the fridge, heating it in a microwave, removing it, and placing it under the coffee-maker spout. The proposal has no acquire/pick-up operation, no remove operation, and no schema defining the compound state 'ready for coffee.'"}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_102045_139402#1", "source": "ALFRED", "nl": "To put the toilet paper away.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1 {\n  ACTION op_find(attr_target=ent_toilet_paper, attr_source=ent_toilet) -> $v2:REF[ENTITY]\n  ACTION op_place(attr_target=$v2, attr_destination=ent_wall_holder)\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Assumes the complete entity category includes `ent_toilet_paper`, `ent_toilet`, and `ent_wall_holder`, as N6 directs. The source location and destination are represented symbolically."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-15345", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "mathematica_code gives wrong output with Max: symbols('x'); mathematica_code(Max(x,2)) should produce Max[x,2] but produces Max(2, x).", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "`code_call` represents both expected `Max[x,2]` and actual `Max(2,x)` only as generic calls; it has no bracket-call versus parenthesis-call syntax, ordered source-file change model, or test/regression schema. Therefore the defining defect is lost."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15098", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified", "nl": "Django i18n_patterns fails for language locales containing both script and region, such as en-Latn-US; modify django/utils/translation/trans_real.py so these locale paths return 200 rather than 404.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "The issue needs imports, assignments, list literals, string literals containing locale tags and URLs, HTTP-status assertions, multiple reproduction steps, expected/actual responses, and a code patch/test. The seven generic code nodes and `attr_reproduction: CODE` cannot encode this report."}, {"item_id": "c5957", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Please provide a recipe for high protein flatbread; change it to one with high reviews; how much protein is in these recipes; how long will this take me to make?", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "This is a multi-turn revision and follow-up. There is no recipe intent, ingredient/recipe schema, review-score attribute, nutrition/protein measurement schema, preparation-time schema, or conversational reference to prior generated recipes."}, {"item_id": "c436", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "What places to visit in Bilbao? Is Guggenheim Museum open everyday? What can I do if it rains? Suggest picturesque towns nearby.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "`int_recommend_places(attr_location=ent_bilbao)` could encode only an initial coarse recommendation. It cannot encode museum opening status, rain contingency, nearby relation, picturesque criterion, or the linked multi-turn sequence."}, {"item_id": "c996", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "What is your take on love? Do you think you will fall in love?", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No intent/schema represents an opinion request, an AI-self-capability question, or the follow-up relationship. N4 disallows retaining the substantive questions as prose."}, {"item_id": "c4939", "source": "PRISM", "nl": "Is marriage important? How did marriage come to the world?", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No opinion, historical-origin, or social-institution explanation intent/schema exists. Encoding this merely as `int_learn_about(attr_topic=ent_marriage)` would lose both distinct questions."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-129421", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Explain in depth, through a composite persona Mark, how linear algebra lets ChatGPT-4 transform text into numbers and numbers into answers, with concrete numerical examples and a casual conversational American style plus 18th-century mathematical illustration.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "There are no categories/schemas for a composite persona, expertise roles, style composition, depth, the bidirectional transformer explanation, equations, or numerical examples. The provided `int_learn_about` is far too coarse."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-148973", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write a hilarious 17+ comedy script in which the Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo react to badly translated sentences, roast errors, and use occasional funny quotes.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No script-generation intent, character/cast relation, comedy register, age-rating constraint, translation-error subject, or dialogue-scene schema is defined. N4 prevents preserving the requested script substance as a text payload."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-142592", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Please write steps for homiletics and engaging Christian sermons.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "Neither a sermon/homiletics artifact schema nor ordered instructional-step, audience-engagement, religious-context, and example relations are present."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-22638", "source": "PATHs", "nl": "Write an email introducing yourself and your job position to another party and asking for collaborations; then provide the recipient's next response.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "`op_send_email` describes an external sending action, not generation of a two-party email exchange. There is no self/job-position/organization/collaboration schema or response-turn generation intent."}, {"item_id": "1775955172098", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "I need help planning a trip to Italy.", "braincode": "ENTRYPOINT $v1\nTASK $v1:PROPOSITION {\n  GENERATE(attr_target=val_trip_plan, attr_specification=int_plan_trip(attr_destination=ent_italy)) -> $v2:PROPOSITION\n  RETURN $v2\n}", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The new planning intent and destination attribute cover the initial request, assuming `val_trip_plan` is an entry in the complete value glossary under N6. Its exact intent schema and required/optional attribute order are not actually supplied."}, {"item_id": "1775612391896", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "For a React and Node.js CRUD job-hunt tracker, should Update use a new page or modal? I want a balance of easy implementation and daily-dashboard UX.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No technical-design-advice, CRUD, React, Node.js, modal/page alternative, comparison criterion, or recommendation schema exists."}, {"item_id": "1775448065619", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Plan a simple three-day high-protein meal prep with breakfast, lunch, and dinner, optimized to save time and eat better.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No meal-plan, meal/course, dietary nutrient, duration, optimization, shopping-list, preparation-step, or ingredient quantity schema exists."}, {"item_id": "1775760283577", "source": "ThoughtTrace", "nl": "Explain end-stage heart failure like to a five-year-old, including how long someone with 20% heart function may live without a transplant.", "braincode": "<no valid BrainCode expression>", "coverage": "Fail", "notes": "No medical-explanation, age-appropriate register, prognosis, probability/range, heart-function percentage, or transplant-condition schema exists. `int_learn_about` would lose the essential question and audience constraint."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "low", "notes": "Anthropic and Gemini disagree on including quantity for the pillows, on cart-addition order, on whether the ALFRED cup task is representable, on Task/GENERATE versus CONVO/UTTER for recipe requests, and materially on the SWE-bench encoding. The supplied SWE expressions are themselves invalid against the proposed grammar in places: `attr_reproduction` receives a list despite its declared CODE type, and Gemini uses malformed spaced NAME literals. These disagreements confirm missing canonical schemas rather than harmless surface variation."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "Types & Lexicon", "change": "Remove COMMENT from BrainCode source or constrain comments so they cannot contain arbitrary natural-language text; N4 forbids the current `COMMENT ::= \"--\" [^\\n]*` prose channel. Replace the URL semantic dependency on RFC-3986 with a complete in-spec lexical and validity grammar, and define all character-set/complement and newline semantics used by lexical productions."}, {"target": "role-symbol, value-symbol, attribute-symbol, operation-symbol, intent-symbol", "change": "Change every open-category membership rule from prefix-only acceptance to: valid exactly when a corresponding member entry exists in the complete glossary. A spelling such as `role_unlisted` or `int_unlisted` must not be a legal expression token merely because it matches a regex."}, {"target": "attribute-symbol and value-symbol", "change": "Implement N3: for every attribute, list its allowed value symbols/categories and any compositional rule, not merely the broad type VALUE. In particular define closed/permitted sets for tone, unit, ordering, currency, state, quality, goal, format, requirements, and failure values; reject values from an unrelated VALUE subcategory."}, {"target": "operation-symbol and intent-symbol", "change": "Give every operation and intent an actual complete ordered schema: required and optional attributes, their precise types/category restrictions, result type selection, duplicate/default behavior, and canonical selection/decomposition rules. Add a rule resolving `op_find`'s REF versus LIST result and define canonical ordering for all intent attributes."}, {"target": "operation-symbol", "change": "Add schemas required by the closed samples: `op_add_to_cart`; acquisition/pick-up and removal/retrieval operations; compatible-with/reference constraints for product selection; and the web/household navigation or state transitions needed to make those actions executable. Specify when a source-location find is sufficient versus acquisition is required."}, {"target": "code-form and SWE-bench coverage", "change": "Replace the seven-node generic code subset with a typed, closed code AST sufficient for sampled issue facts: ordered statement sequences, imports, member/attribute access, indexing/bracket calls versus parenthesized calls, lists/tuples/dictionaries, typed string atoms, URLs/status assertions, file edits, tests, expected/actual behavior, and regression criteria. Give `int_bug_report` a complete schema whose reproduction field accepts the appropriate typed code sequence. Demonstrate full valid symbolic encodings for both sampled SWE-bench reports."}, {"target": "intent-symbol and descriptive vocabulary", "change": "Add compositional symbolic schemas/categories for the missing open-task content rather than treating it as opaque prose: recipes and nutrient/review/time constraints; recommendations with proximity/conditions; opinions and historical explanations; technical explanation and persona/register composition; scripts, cast, scene, humor and rating; sermons and ordered instructional guidance; email-generation and generated reply relations; CRUD design alternatives; meal plans; and medical explanations/prognosis with audience-age constraints."}, {"target": "Conversation and Generate", "change": "Define a canonical representation for conversational requests versus requested generated artifacts, plus references to previously generated artifacts and follow-up questions. It must encode c5957's revision, nutrition question, and time question without prose, and remove the current Task-versus-CONVO ambiguity shown by the independent translations."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Revalidate every worked example against the revised grammar and schemas. In particular, ensure generated target symbols have a declared value entry, operation result types match their schemas, and no example relies on an intent attribute (`attr_tone` on `int_follow_up`, `attr_destination` on `int_plan_trip`) that its intent schema has not defined."}], "logic_issues": ["N1 is still violated because role, value, attribute, operation, and intent membership rules accept any namespace-shaped token without requiring a corresponding glossary entry.", "N4 is violated by arbitrary COMMENT text, which is a direct natural-language channel inside a source expression.", "N3 is violated because attributes typed VALUE accept the undifferentiated open value category; the proposal does not list admissible values or a derivation rule per attribute.", "The URL semantic requirement delegates validity to RFC-3986, contradicting N5's requirement that reading BrainCode not depend on an external natural-language standard.", "`op_find` promises `LIST[REF[ENTITY]] or REF[ENTITY]` without a type-selection rule, leaving valid encodings and subsequent typechecking indeterminate.", "Most intent entries name a meaning but provide no schema at all, despite the grammar allowing arbitrary listed attributes. This creates both under-expression and multiple competing encodings.", "The code-form example uses `int_bug_report` as a code-change payload, but bug-report schemas do not define that as a patch/change representation; generic code_call also cannot distinguish the critical Mathematica syntactic forms.", "The proposed lexical grammar says ontology categories are namespace-disjoint, but the structural glossary aggregates multiple punctuation tokens as single members and does not define a per-token lookup rule for those grouped entries."], "notes_assessment": {"N1": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "The proposal makes substantial progress by adding namespaces, literal forms, and structural symbols, but prefix-only membership rules for role/value/attribute/operation/intent symbols permit tokens absent from the glossary. A direct arbitrary-prose COMMENT production is also legal. Therefore a reader cannot trace every source token to a glossary member or glossary-defined closed production."}, "N2": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "Descriptive categories were added, which is progress, but they do not provide a complete lexicon in the required sense: broad prefix rules stand in for actual members, many necessary categories are absent, and most operation/intent entries lack complete schemas and examples of their descriptive relations."}, "N3": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "The attribute entries state coarse static types such as VALUE, but they do not give each attribute its permissible value set or a precise derivation rule. Consequently `val_comedy` is formally admissible as a tone, unit, or currency despite not being meaningful."}, "N4": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "The language removes STRING and quoted text, but unrestricted comment text remains legal in source. In addition, most substantive sampled requests cannot be symbolically encoded, demonstrating that the replacement ontology is not yet sufficient for the no-prose requirement."}, "N5": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "URL validity is defined by reference to RFC-3986 rather than fully by BrainCode's own specification. Prefix-only ontology admission also leaves symbol meaning dependent on an alleged external complete glossary entry that need not exist."}, "N6": {"status": "partial", "reasoning": "The syntax is now mostly written over categories and simulations appropriately assume missing entities such as toilet paper can belong to a complete entity glossary. However, many failures are missing categories/schemas, not merely missing members, and the categories are not closed by actual glossary membership."}, "N7": {"status": "violated", "reasoning": "The handle rule is helpful, but canonical encoding is not achieved: `op_find` result cardinality is unspecified, intent schemas and argument order are absent, and independent translations diverge substantially on plan shape and construct selection."}, "N8": {"status": "partial", "reasoning": "Structured literals and code nodes attempt to retain richness, but the current ontology is too coarse for the sampled open requests and too weak for both SWE reports. The necessary response is richer compositional symbolic schemas, not restoration of prose payloads."}}, "rebuttal_rulings": [], "chosen_position": null, "position_comparison": null, "reasoning": "The attempt addresses several prior structural requests and passes doc hygiene, but it cannot be accepted because hard notes N1 through N5 and N7 remain violated. In particular, prefix-only namespace rules and unrestricted comments prevent a genuinely closed, prose-free vocabulary, while generic VALUE and incomplete schemas leave encoding non-canonical. The core direction is sound, so the appropriate decision is needs-rework rather than rejection."}
Doc hygiene: All changes carry a complete glossary entry (gloss + worked example), and every revise/remove targets an existing entry.
